<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaMazon U'Mayim Acharonim — Halachah 5<br>Part 2 of 2 (§§ 6–9) — FINAL<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:28</span></span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 (conclusion) — "<i>Vayehi Binso'a HaAron</i>"</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"and it was, when the Aron traveled, Moshe said: 'Rise, Hashem, and let Your enemies be scattered and Your foes flee before You.'"</span> The enemies and foes mentioned above — the <span class="h">misnagdim</span> of truth who are nourished by the fallen Toros — flee and are nullified through the traveling of the <span class="h">Aron</span>. For through this the aforementioned Toros were sorted and elevated, and the nourishment of the enemies and foes was nullified of itself. This is: <span class="v">"and when it rested, he said: 'Return, Hashem, the myriads of the thousands [alfai] of Israel.'"</span> <span class="v">"Alfai"</span> specifically — the aspect of the <span class="h">alafin</span> that return and rest when the <span class="h">Aron</span> rests in its place. Then the <span class="h">alafin</span> that were sorted return and rest in their place in peace, for they were once again made into holy Torah.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — <i>Hachnasas Orchim</i>: Hosting Righteous Guests Restores Fallen Torah</div>

<div class="p">For this very reason, the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> to elevate the fallen Toros mentioned above is through <span class="h">hachnasas orchim</span> — hosting righteous guests — as above. For the righteous guest who walks and travels on the road, who is unsettled and wandering — all his wandering is for the aforementioned purpose: to sort and rectify <span class="h">avira d'alma</span>, which is the fallen Toros. This is <span class="v">"and they followed at Your feet, bearing Your word"</span> — and our Sages expounded: <span class="mx">"these are the talmidai chachamim who go and wear out their feet from city to city, engaging in the word of G-d."</span> All their wandering — wearing out their feet — is for the word of G-d, to sort the aforementioned Toros.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore when a person merits to host this righteous guest, bringing him into his home and honoring him properly — he gives rest to the fallen Toros that the <span class="h">tzadik</span> was engaged in sorting during his road-traveling and wandering. For now, when he enters a house and finds a place to rest, he builds in completeness wondrous structures from the fallen Toros he gathered and sorted on his journey. This is exactly like the resting of the <span class="h">Aron</span> and <span class="h">Mishkan</span>, when it was said: <span class="v">"Return, Hashem, the myriads of the alfai of Israel."</span> For wherever the <span class="h">tzadik</span> goes, the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> and Torah go with him, just as they went with Israel and the <span class="h">Aron</span> in the <span class="h">midbar</span>.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges: one who hosts this guest in his home gives an actual resting place to the Toros that were sorted through him. Therefore specifically through this, one rectifies and elevates the fallen <span class="h">alafin</span>, for they are exactly one aspect. As it is written: <span class="v">"and he went around to Bais El and the Gilgal and the Mitzpah... and his return was to Ramah, for there was his house"</span> (Shmuel I, 7). Our Sages expounded: <span class="mx">"wherever he went, there was his house."</span> For Shmuel went around to all these places to sort the <span class="h">alafin</span> — for this is the purpose of all the <span class="h">tzadikim's</span> wanderings. And this is <span class="v">"his return was to Ramah, for there was his house"</span> — wherever he went, there was his house. For wherever he came, he brought the fallen Toros from <span class="h">avira d'alma</span> into the aspect of "house." Therefore wherever he came — there was his house.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — The <i>Bais HaMikdash</i> and Shlomo; the Corruption That Destroyed It</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential completeness of the <span class="h">tikkun</span> was in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — the essential House standing in its place, <span class="h">baisa bain shamaya l'ar'a</span>. There the essential Torah [resides]. Therefore specifically Shlomo built the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, for Shlomo merited these <span class="h">alafin</span> in holiness — <span class="v">"and he spoke three thousand parables,"</span> etc. — as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span>. Therefore he specifically merited to build the House where the essential permanence of true Torah resides — the aspect of these <span class="h">alafin</span> in holiness.</div>

<div class="p">But <span class="h">ba'avonosainu harabim</span> [through our many sins], even then purification was not yet complete from the Serpent's counsel. The <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> so greatly provoked that afterward he deceived Israel through evil teachings — through Yarav'am and his cohorts and the false prophets, who all drew from the fallen Toros — until sins prevailed and the House was destroyed. For there are many levels in these evil teachings: some twist truth entirely, some deviate slightly, some deviate so minutely it is impossible to detect.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> greatly provokes specifically against <span class="h">talmidai chachamim</span> — even great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> can sometimes err through the fallen Toros that turn their minds from truth. Therefore even Shlomo HaMelech himself — who merited the four <span class="h">alafin</span> in holiness and truly merited to build the House — yet immediately when he began building, the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> was very jealous and provoked him until he too was deceived through evil teachings, until he married Pharaoh's daughter and foreign women. All this came through blemished teachings — he said to Isiel and Uchal (Mishlai 30) and wanted to outsmart the Torah of Moshe which says <span class="v">"he shall not multiply wives."</span> As he later confessed: <span class="v">"for I am more brutish than any man."</span> This caused the destruction of the House, as our Sages said. As Rashi explains on <span class="v">"and Shlomo loved Hashem"</span> — four years before building the House, but once he began building, <span class="v">"and Shlomo married Pharaoh's daughter."</span> <span class="mx">"You find yourself saying: 'for upon My anger and wrath was this city to Me.'"</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential destruction came from the blemish of the fallen <span class="h">alafin</span> — the blemish of Torah whose essence was in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. When <span class="h">kelipos</span> prevailed and deceived again through fallen Toros not yet fully sorted — the House was destroyed. When Shlomo blemished the <span class="h">alafin</span> by marrying Pharaoh's daughter, the essential blemish was to the <span class="h">korban Tamid</span>. For offering the <span class="h">Tamid</span> elevates the four <span class="h">alafin</span>. When he blemished them, he blemished the <span class="h">Tamid</span>. Now see a wonder: our Sages said that when Shlomo married Pharaoh's daughter, she brought him a thousand kinds of music, etc. — and he slept until four hours, and <span class="mx">"at that hour we learned that the Tamid was offered at four hours."</span> The essential blemish reached the offering of the <span class="h">Tamid</span> specifically — for the blemish of the evil teachings that led to marrying Pharaoh's daughter is the opposite of the <span class="h">Tamid</span>, which is the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the <span class="h">alafin</span>. Therefore the sin caused the <span class="h">Tamid's</span> delay specifically. <span class="v">"And the ways of Hashem are straight."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 8 — The Great <i>Tzadikim</i> Called "<i>Alufim</i>": <i>Mikdash Me'at</i> in Exile</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are called <span class="h">alufim</span> [leaders/chiefs], as it is written: <span class="v">"our alufim are laden,"</span> etc. — <span class="v">"the heads of the thousands [alfai] of the children of Israel."</span> For they elevate the <span class="h">alafin</span> mentioned above. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"the charash [craftsman] and the masger [locksmith] — one thousand [elef]"</span> — they were very great and awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who engaged in rectifying the <span class="h">alafin</span>, to rectify the blemish of the destruction that came through this.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it was indeed said of them: <span class="v">"though I scattered them among the nations and dispersed them in the lands, I became for them a mikdash me'at [a small sanctuary]."</span> Our Sages expounded: <span class="mx">"these are the synagogues and study halls."</span> When one merits in exile to spread true Torah among Israel and to establish synagogues and study halls for Torah — a great <span class="h">tikkun</span> is accomplished. Through this one begins to rectify the blemish of the destruction and engages in its rebuilding.</div>

<div class="p">For all the exiles in which Israel was exiled from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, unsettled and wandering in <span class="h">chutz la'Aretz</span> — this is <span class="h">avira d'alma</span>, for they are called <span class="h">nidachim</span> [cast out]: <span class="v">"if your outcast is at the edge of the heavens,"</span> etc. But Hashem's entire intent is for good — so that specifically through this, Israel should sort and elevate the fallen <span class="h">alafin</span>, the fallen Toros, through the power of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation who can sort and elevate the Toros from <span class="h">avira d'alma</span>, from exile and wandering, to the aspect of <span class="h">baisa</span> — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — by establishing holy <span class="h">yeshivos</span> and study halls for Torah, which are <span class="h">mikdash me'at</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the <span class="h">charash</span> and <span class="h">masger</span> were exiled first — so that through their exile specifically, they would return and rectify the <span class="h">alafin</span>. So it is done in every generation during exile through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — until through this the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> will be rebuilt on its foundation, speedily in our days, Amain.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — "<i>V'avarti B'Eretz Mitzrayim — Ani Hashem</i>": The Essence of Torah Without Intermediaries</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"and I will pass through the land of Egypt — I am Hashem. I and not an angel,"</span> etc. For the essential Exodus was to receive the Torah, as it is written: <span class="v">"when you take the people out of Egypt, you shall serve G-d."</span> The essential receiving of Torah depends on sorting the four <span class="h">alafin</span> from which Torah is drawn. All the signs and wonders Moshe performed in Egypt were the beginning of drawing the Torah, for the essential Torah is to reveal His G-dliness in the world. Moshe began this through all the awesome signs and wonders in Egypt — all through the power of the Torah he began to draw at the burning bush, where Hashem revealed Himself and made known His holy Names — the beginning of the Torah's drawing. For <span class="mx">"the entire Torah is the Name of Hashem."</span> <span class="mx">"Through the Torah, Hashem created the world."</span> Therefore through Torah one can change nature and perform awesome wonders — the entire world is conducted through it. Whoever merits Torah can conduct the world as he wishes and perform awesome wonders — the aspect of all Moshe's signs and wonders, all through the Torah he began to draw.</div>

<div class="p">But because there are <span class="h">misnagdim</span> who receive from the fallen Toros of the fallen <span class="h">alafin</span> — from there came the power of Egypt's sorcerers, who also <span class="v">"did the same with their sorcery."</span> Certainly they too have no power except through some nourishment filtering down from the Torah — the source of all life and all the world's conduct. But they receive through <span class="h">kelipos</span>, from the fallen Toros. Through this they tried to prevail against Moshe. Whatever Moshe did through holiness, through the holy Torah's power, they too did by receiving from the fallen Toros. Through this Pharaoh's heart was hardened against sending them — all through receiving from his sorcerers, who received their evil wisdom from the fallen Toros.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore at the time of the culmination of the Exodus, Hashem was compelled, <span class="h">kivyachol</span>, to reveal Himself. For Hashem and the Torah are entirely one. This is: <span class="v">"and I will pass through the land of Egypt — I am Hashem. I and not an angel,"</span> etc. For the essential grip of the fallen Toros exists when the Torah descends and is clothed in the aspect of angels — in the aspect of <span class="h">Metatron</span>, who is comprised of the six orders of <span class="h">Mishnah</span> — where there is a mix of good and evil, pure and impure, etc. There great sorting is needed — to sort the good from the evil, the truth from the falsehood. One who does not merit to sort properly — <span class="mx">"he who did not merit"</span>, etc. — receives the fallen Toros. For in the essence of Torah at its root, which is entirely G-dliness, the <span class="h">kelipos</span> certainly have no grip at all. Only when it descends through angels, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore at the time of the Exodus from Egypt — where the <span class="h">kelipos</span> had great grip, as is known, the aspect of Egypt's sorcerers who drew from the fallen Toros — it was impossible to leave until Hashem Himself was revealed, <span class="h">kivyachol</span>: <span class="v">"and I will pass through the land of Egypt — I am Hashem. I and not an angel, I and not a seraph, I and not an emissary. I am Hashem — I am He and no other."</span></div>

<div class="p">[The Haggadah] counts four times <span class="v">"Ani"</span> [I] — corresponding to the four <span class="h">alafin</span>. For <span class="mx">"there is no 'Ani' without 'Alef'"</span> — Hashem Himself was revealed, which is the aspect of the essence of Torah itself, not clothed in any angel or seraph. For there are many levels in how Torah is clothed. As soon as it is clothed in some aspect of angel or emissary, some nourishment can filter down to the <span class="h">kelipos</span> — exchanging truth, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — the aspect of fallen Toros. For there are many levels in fallen Toros: some completely invert truth, some deviate slightly, and some deviate so minutely it is impossible to detect at all.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it is impossible to leave the aspect of the exile of Egypt — which encompasses all exiles, physical and spiritual — except by meriting the Torah of Hashem in absolute truth, in the aspect of <span class="v">"and I will pass through the land of Egypt"</span> — and the Targum renders: <span class="mx">"and I will reveal Myself."</span> <span class="v">"I and not an angel, I and not a seraph, I am He and not an emissary, I am Hashem, I am He and no other"</span> — that the Torah should have no grip from "another" at all, but should be in the aspect of <span class="v">"I am Hashem, I am He and no other."</span> For <span class="mx">"Hashem and His Torah are entirely one."</span></div>

<div class="ts">
<div class="sh">✦ Summary — BhM Halachah 5 ✦</div>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>Core teaching</strong> (<span class="lr">LM I:28</span>): Four fallen <em>alafin</em> → fallen Toros → <em>misnagdim</em>. Rectified through <em>Chesed</em> → hosting righteous guests → <em>korban Tamid</em> (gematria = 4 <em>alafin</em>).</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§§1-2</strong>: <em>Birchas HaMazon</em> sorts food from <em>pesoless</em> of fallen Toros clinging since Adam's sin. Four <em>berachos</em> = four <em>alafin</em>. "Four who opened with <em>af</em>" = <em>alef</em> became <em>af</em> (wrath) through blemished teaching.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§3</strong>: <em>HaZan</em> = <em>Chesed</em>. <em>HaAretz</em> = <em>hachnasas orchim</em>/holiness of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. <em>Boneh Yerushalayim</em> = <em>Bais HaMikdash</em> = <em>baisa bain shamaya l'ar'a</em> = permanence of true Torah. <em>HaTov V'HaMaitiv</em> = sorting good from <em>misnagdim</em> (slain of Baitar).</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§§4-5</strong>: Pray to find truth. <em>Mishkan</em> traveled through <em>midbar</em> to sort fallen Toros from <em>avira d'alma</em>. Each journey = Torah. "Return, Hashem, <em>alfai</em> Yisrael" = <em>alafin</em> restored.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§§6-7</strong>: <em>Hachnasas orchim</em> gives rest to the <em>tzadik's</em> sorted Toros. Shmuel: "wherever he went, his house." Shlomo built the <em>Bais HaMikdash</em> but blemished the <em>alafin</em> → married Pharaoh's daughter → delayed the <em>Tamid</em> → destruction.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>§§8-9</strong>: <em>Tzadikim</em> = <em>alufim</em>. Exile's purpose: sort fallen Toros through establishing study halls = <em>mikdash me'at</em>. The Exodus required "I and not an angel" = Torah in its essence, not clothed in intermediaries where <em>kelipos</em> can grip. Four times <em>Ani</em> = four <em>alafin</em>.</p>
